## ============================================================================
## Failure modes — one step fails on purpose
## ============================================================================
## Handy for demos: shows the red `✗`, the failing assertion message, and
## how the run keeps going unless `--fail-fast` is set.
## ============================================================================

### 1.1 Passes cleanly
GET {{jsonplaceholder}}/posts/1

> {%
    client.test("is a real post", function() {
        client.assert(response.status === 200, "nope");
        client.assert(response.body.id === 1, "wrong id");
    });
%}

### 1.2 Intentionally fails a test
GET {{jsonplaceholder}}/posts/1

> {%
    client.test("title contains 'not-actually-in-the-title'", function() {
        client.assert(
            response.body.title.includes("not-actually-in-the-title"),
            "substring not found: title was " + JSON.stringify(response.body.title)
        );
    });
%}

### 1.3 Hits a URL that returns 404
GET {{jsonplaceholder}}/posts/9999999

> {%
    client.test("server said this exists", function() {
        client.assert(response.status === 200, "got " + response.status + " instead");
    });
%}

### 1.4 Passes again — shows that the suite keeps going
GET {{jsonplaceholder}}/users/{{default_user_id}}

> {%
    client.test("user returned", function() {
        client.assert(response.status === 200, "no user");
        client.assert(typeof response.body.email === "string", "no email");
    });
%}
